<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories was the Nazi German ministry created in July 1941 under Alfred Rosenberg to govern Soviet lands placed under civil administration. It oversaw the Reichskommissariats of Ostland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, though the SS, the army and the commissioners often acted on their own. Its administration took part in the plunder, forced labour and mass murder carried out in these territories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reich-ministry-for-the-occupied-eastern-territories.jpg" alt="Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RsVe. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsadler_Deutsches_Reich_(1935%E2%80%931945).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> first spoke of an Eastern Ministry on 3 March 1941, in a conversation with Wilhelm Keitel about the planned invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, holding that the political tasks there were too difficult to leave to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>. On 26 March Reinhard Heydrich noted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>'s suggestion that it be given to Alfred Rosenberg, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>'s chief racial theorist and a Baltic German. After the invasion of 22 June 1941, Hitler set up the ministry on 17 July, though it was not announced to the public until November. Its deputy minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-meyer">Alfred Meyer</a>, later represented Rosenberg at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wannsee-conference">Wannsee Conference</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rosenberg planned four Reichskommissariats. Ostland, covering the Baltic countries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, was set up at once under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinrich-lohse">Hinrich Lohse</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on 20 August 1941 under Erich Koch; Moskowien and Kaukasien were never established, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> failed to hold those regions firmly. The ministry shared in drawing up and carrying out Generalplan Ost and was one of the main agencies that oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>Rosenberg hoped to win over the non-Jewish population by presenting the Germans as liberators, and a land reform of February 1942 promised to abolish the collective farms. Göring insisted they be kept under another name, and Hitler called the redistribution stupid. The SS and its auxiliary police acted as they wished, Hitler ordered Koch to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> harshly, and the ministry had no authority over the army or the security forces. By late 1944 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> had retaken the territories, and the Flensburg Government abolished the ministry on 5 May 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its staff wore brown uniforms like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> and SA officials but with a dark brown collar. The colour of the collar tabs showed the level of office, from the district commissariats to the Reich Commissariat and the ministry itself, and rank was shown by four-pointed stars of a special pattern and by borders of oak leaves, embroidered in gold for the senior leadership. The minister's tabs carried a large eagle with lowered wings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the invasion of the Soviet Union in 1941, Germany placed the occupied Baltic states and western Belarus under a civil administration, Reichskommissariat Ostland, created in July 1941 under Alfred Rosenberg's <b>Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a></li></ul></section>
